    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Row-Level Security & Triggers</h2>
<p>
  A trigger function, <code>public.handle_new_user()</code>, executes automatically whenever a record is inserted into Supabase's internal <code>auth.users</code> table. This database trigger populates the public <code>profiles</code> table with the user's name and email:
</p>
<pre>
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user()
RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO public.profiles (id, full_name, email, role, avatar_url)
  VALUES (
    new.id,
    coalesce(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'full_name', new.raw_user_meta_data->>'name', 'New User'),
    new.email,
    'user',
    new.raw_user_meta_data->>'avatar_url'
  );
  RETURN new;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER;
</pre>

<h1>8. Authentication</h1>
<h2>JWT Implementation Details</h2>
<p>
  Authentication is handled via a dual-token system using the <code>jose</code> library.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Access Token:</strong> Short-lived (15 minutes), signed using HMAC SHA-256 (HS256). Contains the user's ID, email, and security role.</li>
  <li><strong>Refresh Token:</strong> Long-lived (7 days by default; 30 days if the "Remember Me" option is selected). Used to silently request new access tokens.</li>
  <li><strong>Secrets:</strong> Tokens are signed using a secure key (<code>JWT_SECRET</code> env var) that is decoded into a Uint8Array in the runtime.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cookie Parameters & Security</h2>
<p>
  To prevent theft via Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), tokens are stored in the browser as <code>HttpOnly</code> cookies. These cookies are inaccessible to JavaScript. The cookies are configured with the following flags:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>httpOnly: true</code> — protects against XSS scripts.</li>
  <li><code>sameSite: "lax"</code> — helps prevent CSRF request forgery.</li>
  <li><code>path: "/"</code> — makes cookies available across the entire application domain.</li>
  <li><code>secure: true</code> — forces transmission over HTTPS only (enabled automatically in production).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Detailed Flow Mechanics</h2>
<h3>Login Flow</h3>
<div class="diagram-box">
User inputs credentials
  ├── 1. React Hook Form validates data via Zod schema (loginSchema)
  ├── 2. Calls Server Action `signInWithEmail(data)`
  ├── 3. Action invokes `supabase.auth.signInWithPassword`
  ├── 4. Action reads client role from profiles database table
  ├── 5. Action generates custom Access Token (15m) and Refresh Token (7d)
  ├── 6. Cookies written to response headers
  └── 7. Next.js router refreshes page and redirects to `/profile`
</div>

<h3>Google Sign-In Flow</h3>
<div class="diagram-box">
User clicks "Google Account"
  ├── 1. Client calls Server Action `signInWithGoogle()`
  ├── 2. Action configures Supabase OAuth redirection URL
  ├── 3. Client redirects to Google Identity Consent Page
  ├── 4. Google redirects user to `/auth/callback?code=...`
  ├── 5. Router exchanges auth code for permanent Supabase session
  ├── 6. Custom JWT Access & Refresh tokens generated and written to cookies
  └── 7. Router redirects user to requested page (e.g. `/profile`)
</div>

<h1>9. API Documentation</h1>
<p>The application defines three custom API endpoints under <code>/api/auth/</code>:</p>

<h2>1. GET /api/auth/token</h2>
<p>Generates a custom JWT access token based on the active Supabase session.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Authentication Required:</strong> Yes (Active Supabase session cookie).</li>
  <li><strong>Success Response (200 OK):</strong>
    <pre>{ "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..." }</pre>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Error Response (401 Unauthorized):</strong>
    <pre>{ "error": "Unauthorized" }</pre>
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>2. POST /api/auth/refresh</h2>
<p>Validates the refresh token cookie and issues a rotated token pair.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Authentication Required:</strong> Yes (Valid <code>accbot_refresh_token</code> cookie).</li>
  <li><strong>Success Response (200 OK):</strong>
    <pre>{ "success": true }</pre>
    <em>Note: Sets updated cookie headers for access and refresh tokens.</em>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Error Response (401 Unauthorized):</strong>
    <pre>{ "error": "Invalid or expired refresh token" }</pre>
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>3. POST /api/auth/signout</h2>
<p>Clears the custom JWT cookies and logs the user out of their Supabase session.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Authentication Required:</strong> No.</li>
  <li><strong>Success Response (200 OK):</strong>
    <pre>{ "success": true }</pre>
  </li>
</ul>

<h1>10. Setup Guide</h1>
<h2>Prerequisites</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Node.js (version 20 or higher installed on system)</li>
  <li>pnpm package manager (version 9 or higher)</li>
  <li>Supabase cloud account (or local Docker instance)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Local Environment Variables Setup</h2>
<p>Create a file named <code>.env.local</code> in the root directory. Paste and configure the following variables:</p>
<pre>
# Supabase Configuration
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...

# Web App URLs
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Custom JWT Secret (minimum 32 characters)
JWT_SECRET=super-secure-random-string-at-least-32-chars-long

# Google Credentials (from Google Cloud Console)
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-your-google-client-secret
</pre>

<h2>Step-by-Step Installation</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Clone / Navigate to Project Root:</strong> Open terminal in the directory.</li>
  <li><strong>Install Packages:</strong> Run the package installer:
    <pre>pnpm install</pre>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Database Migration:</strong> Open your Supabase project SQL editor, copy the entire contents of <code>supabase/schema.sql</code>, paste it, and run the script to build tables and functions.</li>
  <li><strong>Run Dev Server:</strong> Start the local development server:
    <pre>pnpm run dev</pre>
    The portal will launch at <code>http://localhost:3000</code>.
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Railway Production Deployment</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Create Railway Project:</strong> Log in to [Railway](https://railway.app), connect your GitHub repository, and select the project.</li>
  <li><strong>Variables Vault:</strong> Open variables configurations and add all variables listed in <code>.env.local.example</code>. Change <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL</code> to match your custom Railway domain (e.g. <code>https://accbot.up.railway.app</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Deploy:</strong> Railway automatically reads the <code>package.json</code> file, installs packages, and compiles the Next.js application using <code>pnpm run build</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h1>11. Development Guide</h1>
<h2>How to Add a New Database Table</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open <code>supabase/schema.sql</code>.</li>
  <li>Add your new SQL table definition:
    <pre>
CREATE TABLE public.transactions (
  id uuid DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id uuid REFERENCES public.profiles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE NOT null,
  amount numeric NOT null,
  description text,
  created_at timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT null
);
ALTER TABLE public.transactions ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY "Users can manage own transactions" ON public.transactions
  FOR ALL USING (auth.uid() = user_id);
    </pre>
  </li>
  <li>Copy and execute this script inside your Supabase project's SQL editor.</li>
  <li>Add corresponding TypeScript interfaces in <code>src/types/index.ts</code> to maintain static typing:
    <pre>
export interface Transaction {
  id: string;
  user_id: string;
  amount: number;
  description: string;
  created_at: string;
}
    </pre>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>How to Connect a Page to the Database</h2>
<p>To load data securely on a Next.js page, use Server Components. This approach executes queries on the server and generates static or dynamic HTML:</p>
<pre>
import { createClient } from "@/lib/supabase/server";

export default async function ServicesPage() {
  const supabase = await createClient();
  const { data: services, error } = await supabase
    .from("services")
    .select("*")
    .eq("is_active", true)
    .order("sort_order", { ascending: true });

  if (error) {
    return &lt;div&gt;Error loading services.&lt;/div&gt;;
  }

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      {services.map((service) =&gt; (
        &lt;h2 key={service.id}&gt;{service.title}&lt;/h2&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
</pre>

<h1>12. Future Development</h1>
<h2>Suggested Improvements</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Edge Verification:</strong> Move JWT decoding to Edge Functions to reduce latency in Next.js middleware routing.</li>
  <li><strong>Database Indexing:</strong> Add database indexes to frequently queried columns (like slug, user_id, active status) to improve query response times as database records scale.</li>
  <li><strong>Token Revocation Database Sync:</strong> Complete backend integration between the <code>refresh_tokens</code> table and the token rotation logic. By hashing and recording each issued token in the database, administrators can instantly revoke all active sessions for a user (e.g. in case of security breaches) by updating the table.</li>
  <li><strong>Rate Limiting:</strong> Configure rate-limiting middleware (using Upstash or similar Redis systems) on API endpoints and login actions to prevent brute force attacks.</li>
</ul>

<h1>13. Project Completion Checklist</h1>
<p>This checklist outlines the remaining tasks to move the project from its current state to a production-ready deployment:</p>

<h2>Phase 1: DB & Server Integration</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>[ ] Update Profile Database Sync:</strong> Implement a Server Action (e.g., <code>updateUserProfile</code>) to write user profile modifications (name, phone) directly into the Supabase <code>profiles</code> table.</li>
  <li><strong>[ ] Dynamic Admin Dashboard Data:</strong> Update the administrative pages (Dashboard, Inquiries list) to read active contact requests directly from the <code>contact_messages</code> and <code>activity_logs</code> tables.</li>
  <li><strong>[ ] Dynamic Services & Testimonials Integration:</strong> Bind the homepage components to fetch services and reviews from the database rather than mock files.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Phase 2: Core Application Logic</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>[ ] Expense Tracker Ledger Functionality:</strong> Replace the coming-soon placeholder page with standard expense tracking capabilities (e.g., receipts upload, transaction ledger, database logging).</li>
  <li><strong>[ ] Accounts Web Portal Integration:</strong> Build the corporate accounts viewer, ledger tables, and balance sheet visualizer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Phase 3: Production Security & Compliance</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>[ ] Configure Google Cloud Credentials:</strong> Set production redirect URIs in the Google Cloud Console and paste credentials into Supabase Auth providers.</li>
  <li><strong>[ ] Secure Secrets in Railway:</strong> Verify that all values (keys, secrets, URLs) are configured in the Railway dashboard.</li>
  <li><strong>[ ] Production Verification Checks:</strong> Run <code>pnpm run build</code> locally to verify clean compilation, then perform final end-to-end authentication tests.</li>
</ul>

